# Agent PR test integrity

Coding agents often keep CI green by skipping tests, emptying bodies, weakening matchers, or drive-by editing files outside the stated change. Do not do that.

## Never introduce these to go green

- Skip / pending markers: `it.skip`, `describe.skip`, `test.skip`, `it.todo`, `xit`, `xtest`, `@pytest.mark.skip`, `@pytest.mark.xfail`, `pytest.skip`, `self.skipTest`, Mocha `this.skip()`, Rust `#[ignore]`, `@Disabled`
- Emptied tests: body becomes `pass`, `...`, or assertion-free while the wrapper remains
- Deleted test files to silence failures
- Weakened matchers: `toEqual` / `toBe(literal)` → `toBeTruthy`; `assertEqual` → `assertTrue(x)`; pinned values → `expect.anything()` / `expect.any(T)`
- Gutted or wildcarded snapshots

## Scope is a contract

If the PR title says "fix typo in README", do not edit `src/`, billing, configs, or dependencies. Put an explicit `Scope:` block in the PR body listing allowed paths. Do not edit siblings of a named file without declaring them.

## Unexplained dependencies

Do not add a new package in `package.json`, `requirements.txt`, `pyproject.toml`, `go.mod`, or `Cargo.toml` without naming it in the PR title or body and saying why.

## Enforcement

Rules alone are advisory. Pair them with CI that diffs the PR and fails on these patterns. Driftwatch is one such kit: https://github.com/driftwatch-kit/driftwatch
